    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d7">

                <pb facs="0040" n="30" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg30"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">390.1115A/1–945: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hurley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-01-09T15:00:00+07:00">January 9, 1945—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 3:07 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>25. Embassy’s telegram 81 to Delhi<anchor n="32" xml:id="d7-f1"/> referred to
                    Government personnel only (Department’s 34, January 6<anchor n="33"
                        xml:id="d7-f2"/>). Embassy is continuing endeavors to facilitate withdrawal
                    from China of non-essential Americans (Embassy’s 3, January 2). This program has
                    been retarded due to temporary stabilization of military situation and it is
                    feared would be completely nullified were additional civilians other than
                    Government personnel authorized to proceed to China except in cases of special
                    emergency.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hurley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
